  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 110 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nTemperatures will increase on Thursday as southwest flow aloft\nreturns. All areas will be at or above normal. A few south to\nsouthwest breezes will be noted in the afternoon, otherwise a\nbeautiful day will be in store. \n\nAnother shortwave will approach the Four Corners on Friday then \ncross northern NM on Saturday. This system will bring less \nprecipitation than tonight's system, but should bring a bit more \nwind. Precipitation looks to be focused across northwest NM \nFriday and Friday night, and diminishing by noon Saturday. Snow \nlevels will be a bit lower -- starting around 8kft and lowering to\nnear 6500 feet. However, snowfall amounts will be minimal once \nagain. Meanwhile, H7 winds on Friday will increase to between \n30-45kt. While not all of this momentum will reach the surface, \nmixing heights do look to be quite high for this time of year. \nThus, widespread breezy to windy southwest winds are expected. \nWind speeds have trended down slightly from yesterday, but gusts \nbetween 35 and 45 mph appear likely. Surface winds will switch \naround to the west and northwest on Saturday as the trough passes.\nBreezy to locally windy conditions will persist on Saturday. \n\nQuiet conditions are in store for Sunday with shortwave ridging\naloft. Another storm system is in the offing for Monday. Models\nare in better agreement today, though not perfect, that a \nshortwave trough will cross the state which will bring light \nprecipitation to the area. The GFS/GEFS show both a faster and\ndeeper system than the EC/EC Ensemble, but both indicate at least\na little precipitation.",
